{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 34, :comments ["<b>And Esau despised.</b> This too concerns the birthright, because he saw that his father had no wealth. Many will wonder, for Abraham left him much property — as though they have not seen in their days a great rich man in his youth who came to poverty in his old age. The proof is that Isaac his father loved Esau on account of his need; and if bread were plentiful in his father's house and he was honored in his eyes, he would not have sold his birthright for a stew; and if his father ate delicacies every day, why did he say \"Bring me game,\" and why did Jacob not have precious garments, and why did his mother not give him silver and gold for the way, when he says \"and give me bread to eat and garments to wear,\" and why did she not send him wealth though she loved him, so that he needed to tend the sheep. And the verse that says \"and the man grew great\" (Genesis 26:13) is before his old age. And the foolhardy will think that wealth is a great elevation for the righteous — but Elijah refutes them. And further they will ask: why did HaShem withhold property from Isaac? Perhaps they will inform us why He withheld the light of his eyes. And let them not fob us off with the reed of derash, for it has a sod, and it is not for us to seek it, for deep are the thoughts of HaShem, and there is no power in human intellect to comprehend them. And so others said: behold, he has sheep, for Rebekah said to him \"Go now to the sheep\" (Genesis 27:9), and it is possible that a little of his flocks remained to him. Also fitting is the interpretation of \"go now to the sheep\" — to the place of the sheep where they are sold."], :book :genesis, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "Resolved השם as HaShem per divine-name protocol; עורי לב rendered literally as 'the foolhardy' (lit. 'those whose heart is awake/alert'), reading it as a pejorative idiom."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"] ["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:45:57.832682887Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}